WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.300
Under a microscope, a drop of pond water may show a Paramecium.

2
00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.160
It is a single cell, shaped like a slipper.

3
00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:15.440
This one cell swims, catches food, pumps out extra water, and splits in two.

4
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:19.560
Your body does the same kinds of jobs, with trillions of cells.

5
00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:22.140
So how can one cell survive alone?

6
00:00:23.183 --> 00:00:26.983
A unicellular organism is a living thing made of one cell.

7
00:00:27.703 --> 00:00:32.563
A Paramecium swims with cilia, short hair-like structures that beat together like

8
00:00:32.563 --> 00:00:37.043
oars. Food is enclosed in food vacuoles, where it is digested.

9
00:00:38.003 --> 00:00:39.723
Water keeps moving in by osmosis.

10
00:00:40.583 --> 00:00:45.243
A contractile vacuole, a sac, fills with that extra water and squeezes it out.

11
00:00:45.243 --> 00:00:49.163
Last, binary fission: one cell divides into two.

12
00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:53.373
A multicellular organism is made of many cells.

13
00:00:54.193 --> 00:00:58.033
A specialized cell has a structure that suits it to a particular job.

14
00:00:59.273 --> 00:01:02.213
Similar cells working together form a tissue, such as muscle.

15
00:01:03.293 --> 00:01:05.713
Several tissues form an organ, such as the heart.

16
00:01:06.953 --> 00:01:10.573
Related organs form an organ system, such as the circulatory system.

17
00:01:11.567 --> 00:01:14.567
Each specialized cell has a shape that fits its job.

18
00:01:15.447 --> 00:01:18.807
A nerve cell has a long extension that carries signals far.

19
00:01:19.727 --> 00:01:22.207
Some human nerve cells reach about a metre long.

20
00:01:23.087 --> 00:01:27.567
A red blood cell is a small, flexible, dented disc, so it bends through narrow

21
00:01:27.567 --> 00:01:32.987
vessels. A root hair cell has a long tube that touches the soil, where water enters.

22
00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:36.130
Do these cells carry different instructions?

23
00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:42.950
Mostly no. Nearly every body cell with a nucleus carries essentially the same DNA.

24
00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:48.930
What differs is gene expression, the use of a gene's instructions to make a product,

25
00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:50.150
such as a protein.

26
00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:55.890
A muscle cell uses the genes for proteins that slide past each other to make the

27
00:01:55.890 --> 00:02:00.690
cell shorten. A red blood cell in the making uses the genes for hemoglobin.

28
00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:05.090
A mature red blood cell is an exception: it has no nucleus.

29
00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:08.130
So how can one cell survive alone?

30
00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:13.210
A Paramecium holds the structures for every job inside its one cell.

31
00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:16.690
Your body shares those jobs among specialized cells.

32
00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:18.670
Their shapes fit their work.

33
00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:23.250
Nearly all carry the same DNA, but each kind uses different genes.
